<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aegidius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–464/465</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aegidius was a Roman general who commanded in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> from about 457 until his death in 464 or 465. Appointed by Emperor Majorian, he refused to accept Majorian's murder in 461 and ruled the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> independently of the Italian court, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> in 463. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a> inherited his power in what historians call the Domain of Soissons.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus of Panium</a>, Aegidius was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and he is likely to have belonged to an old aristocratic family. Modern scholars propose that it was the family known as the Syagrii, from the name of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a>, although there is no strong evidence. Priscus reports that he served under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> when Aetius was magister militum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, as did the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> and Majorian, both also of Gallo-Roman origin. Priscus adds that Aegidius had considerable influence with Majorian.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> claims that Aegidius was made magister militum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> after Majorian became emperor, and many scholars place this around 458. Paulinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a> describes him leading Roman forces in a besieged city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhone</a>, which many historians take to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, held against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii">Theodoric II</a>. The date and place are uncertain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Ricimer had Majorian killed in 461 and set up Libius Severus, Aegidius refused to recognise the new emperor. He may have pledged allegiance directly to the Eastern emperor Leo I. Priscus notes that the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> feared his army, but his conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> kept him from invading Italy. In 463 he repulsed an invasion by the Aquitanian Goths at the Battle of Orleans, where his forces, apparently largely Frankish, killed Frederic, the brother of Theodoric. He also besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a> at an unknown date.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> reports a story that Aegidius became king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> while the Frankish king Childeric was in exile. Modern scholars are sceptical that a Roman could be called rex of the Franks, but several think it may reflect a real memory. Guy Halsall suggested that Aegidius took the Frankish title to legitimise his position after refusing Severus. Hydatius records that in May 464 Aegidius sent an embassy by sea to the Vandal king Gaiseric, probably to find an ally against Ricimer. He also records a lasting enmity with the Gallo-Roman Agrippinus, whom Aegidius had accused of planning to hand parts of the empire to barbarians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a> writes that Aegidius died soon after an eclipse dated to 464, some saying by ambush or treachery and others by poison, without naming anyone responsible. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> then invaded the regions he had been defending in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syagrius">Syagrius</a> succeeded him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> describes Syagrius as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, which Aegidius had held before him. Syagrius was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>, the son of Childeric, in the 480s, a major step in Clovis's creation of a unified Frankish state. The sources give Aegidius many contradictory titles, among them magister militum, rex, comes and, in one version of the Liber Historiae Francorum, a Roman tyrant.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aegidius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 457 the emperor Majorian appointed <b>Aegidius</b> as commander of the Roman army in Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-soissons">Domain of Soissons</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lugdunum, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Augusta Suessionum, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=464" aria-label="Lugdunum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4664 551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aegidius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=464">What was here in 464 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
